1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:01,176
William Farquhar:

2
00:00:01,256 --> 00:00:05,025
The Co-Founder Singapore
Wrote Out of Its Own Story

3
00:00:05,025 --> 00:00:10,087
At the foot of Fort Canning Green sits
a small garden planted with gambier,

4
00:00:10,167 --> 00:00:14,406
guava, wild nutmeg and rattan,
each one framed like a picture —

5
00:00:14,486 --> 00:00:17,833
a deliberate echo of a set
of watercolour drawings

6
00:00:17,913 --> 00:00:22,575
commissioned two centuries ago by
the man the garden is named after.

7
00:00:22,575 --> 00:00:25,314
Almost nobody who walks through it could

8
00:00:25,394 --> 00:00:28,425
tell you who William
Farquhar actually was.

9
00:00:28,425 --> 00:00:32,375
He ran Singapore for nearly
four and a half years.

10
00:00:32,375 --> 00:00:35,412
Stamford Raffles, whose
name is now more or less

11
00:00:35,492 --> 00:00:37,944
synonymous with the country's founding,

12
00:00:38,024 --> 00:00:41,725
was physically present for a
total of about eight months.

13
00:00:41,725 --> 00:00:44,370
That imbalance alone would be an odd

14
00:00:44,450 --> 00:00:47,175
footnote in most national histories.

15
00:00:47,175 --> 00:00:51,470
In Singapore's case, it produced
a founding myth with one name on

16
00:00:51,550 --> 00:00:55,925
it and a man who built the actual
settlement all but written out.

17
00:00:55,925 --> 00:01:02,070
Farquhar arrived in Malacca in 1795
as an East India Company officer,

18
00:01:02,150 --> 00:01:04,777
became its Commandant in 1803,

19
00:01:04,857 --> 00:01:10,450
and by 1812 held the combined
post of Commandant and Resident.

20
00:01:10,450 --> 00:01:13,435
He held it for the better
part of two decades,

21
00:01:13,515 --> 00:01:18,366
transforming the colony's finances,
pushing through anti-slavery measures,

22
00:01:18,446 --> 00:01:23,097
and earning consistent praise across
nine successive Penang governors —

23
00:01:23,177 --> 00:01:27,775
a run of institutional trust that's
hard to overstate for the period.

24
00:01:27,775 --> 00:01:31,675
He was also, by inclination, a naturalist.

25
00:01:31,675 --> 00:01:35,898
Over his years in Malacca he
commissioned Chinese artists to

26
00:01:35,978 --> 00:01:40,560
produce detailed watercolours of
the region's plants and animals:

27
00:01:40,640 --> 00:01:44,576
477 drawings in total, now
known as the William Farquhar

28
00:01:44,656 --> 00:01:48,592
Collection of Natural History
Drawings, and about as far

29
00:01:48,672 --> 00:01:52,975
from a soldier-administrator's
usual paper trail as it gets.

30
00:01:52,975 --> 00:01:57,137
It's this same collection the
Farquhar Garden at Fort Canning is

31
00:01:57,217 --> 00:02:01,445
built to echo — plants he found
worth recording, framed again two

32
00:02:01,525 --> 00:02:05,900
centuries later for visitors who
mostly don't know the connection.

33
00:02:05,900 --> 00:02:09,514
When Britain returned Malacca
to the Dutch under the

34
00:02:09,594 --> 00:02:13,136
1815 Congress of Vienna,
Penang's merchants worried

35
00:02:13,216 --> 00:02:16,191
openly about losing
their trading position,

36
00:02:16,271 --> 00:02:20,675
and Farquhar was drawn into the
search for a replacement base.

37
00:02:20,675 --> 00:02:24,112
In August 1818 he personally negotiated

38
00:02:24,192 --> 00:02:27,800
a trade treaty with the Sultan of Johor.

39
00:02:27,800 --> 00:02:31,385
When the Carimon Islands
turned out unsuitable,

40
00:02:31,465 --> 00:02:36,300
it was Farquhar who proposed
Singapore instead, in early 1819.

41
00:02:36,300 --> 00:02:40,105
The Governor-General in Bengal
put him in charge of any

42
00:02:40,185 --> 00:02:44,000
new post that came of it —
but subordinate to Raffles.

43
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:49,157
Raffles landed on the 28th of
January 1819, exploited a succession

44
00:02:49,237 --> 00:02:53,442
dispute to install Sultan
Hussein on the Johor throne,

45
00:02:53,522 --> 00:02:58,600
and signed the treaty securing
Singapore on the 6th of February.

46
00:02:58,600 --> 00:03:01,075
He left the very next day.

47
00:03:01,075 --> 00:03:04,176
Farquhar stayed on as
Resident and Commandant,

48
00:03:04,256 --> 00:03:08,750
and would remain in that post for
the next four and a half years.

49
00:03:08,750 --> 00:03:12,819
What Farquhar built in that
time is not really in dispute,

50
00:03:12,899 --> 00:03:15,475
even if it's rarely put on a plaque.

51
00:03:15,475 --> 00:03:20,236
He cleared more than 650,000
square yards of jungle and swamp,

52
00:03:20,316 --> 00:03:23,516
built reservoirs, roads and defence works,

53
00:03:23,596 --> 00:03:26,875
and set up Singapore's first police force.

54
00:03:26,875 --> 00:03:31,019
He commissioned the island's
first proper survey and drew

55
00:03:31,099 --> 00:03:35,021
up its first town plan in
1821 — a document that still

56
00:03:35,101 --> 00:03:39,325
shapes how the old core of
the city reads on a map today.

57
00:03:39,325 --> 00:03:44,926
He rediscovered and charted the deep-water
harbour later renamed Keppel Harbour,

58
00:03:45,006 --> 00:03:48,547
corresponded with regional
rulers to draw in trade,

59
00:03:48,627 --> 00:03:52,675
and persuaded merchant families
to relocate from Malacca.

60
00:03:52,675 --> 00:03:56,452
He granted asylum to
Prince Belawa and some 500

61
00:03:56,532 --> 00:04:00,225
Bugis refugees fleeing
Dutch-controlled Rhio.

62
00:04:00,225 --> 00:04:03,031
He backed the settlement's
first school and

63
00:04:03,111 --> 00:04:06,400
served as president of
the Singapore Institution.

64
00:04:06,400 --> 00:04:11,660
By late 1821 the population
had grown to roughly 5,000,

65
00:04:11,740 --> 00:04:14,750
with commerce already thriving.

66
00:04:14,750 --> 00:04:18,514
None of it happened because
Raffles was managing it from

67
00:04:18,594 --> 00:04:22,850
Bengal and Bencoolen — it happened
because Farquhar was there.

68
00:04:22,850 --> 00:04:28,150
The two men did clash on real
substance, not just personality.

69
00:04:28,150 --> 00:04:32,605
Raffles ordered Farquhar to
introduce opium revenue licences —

70
00:04:32,685 --> 00:04:36,848
a policy Raffles' own supporters
later blamed on Farquhar,

71
00:04:36,928 --> 00:04:41,975
despite Raffles having authorised
it and taken a personal commission.

72
00:04:41,975 --> 00:04:46,042
When merchants objected that
Raffles' planned European quarter at

73
00:04:46,122 --> 00:04:50,126
East Beach was unsuited to trade,
Farquhar pragmatically allowed

74
00:04:50,206 --> 00:04:53,124
temporary warehouses on
the north bank instead;

75
00:04:53,204 --> 00:04:58,548
Raffles later characterised this as outright
disobedience, although it was Farquhar's

76
00:04:58,628 --> 00:05:02,775
approach that kept the young
settlement's commerce from stalling.

77
00:05:02,775 --> 00:05:07,068
Underneath the specific disputes
sat a genuine difference in

78
00:05:07,148 --> 00:05:11,375
how each man saw the place:
Raffles wanted a British port.

79
00:05:11,375 --> 00:05:14,315
Farquhar, working from
the original treaty,

80
00:05:14,395 --> 00:05:18,750
still saw Singapore as sitting
within a Malay political order.

81
00:05:18,750 --> 00:05:23,925
Raffles returned in October 1822,
after three and a half years away,

82
00:05:24,005 --> 00:05:28,100
and at first praised how
far the settlement had come.

83
00:05:28,100 --> 00:05:30,025
That didn't last.

84
00:05:30,025 --> 00:05:33,827
He excluded Farquhar from a
newly formed Town Committee,

85
00:05:33,907 --> 00:05:37,017
took over the weekly
Resident's court himself,

86
00:05:37,097 --> 00:05:40,968
and wrote to Bengal accusing
Farquhar of overspending and

87
00:05:41,048 --> 00:05:45,000
improper land dealings —
charges he would later withdraw.

88
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:48,852
On the 11th of January
1823 Raffles wrote that

89
00:05:48,932 --> 00:05:52,950
he didn't consider Farquhar
capable of the job.

90
00:05:52,950 --> 00:05:55,935
The very next day, in
a letter to his cousin,

91
00:05:56,015 --> 00:05:59,954
he was celebrating Singapore's
rise as "a great emporium" —

92
00:06:00,034 --> 00:06:04,325
the emporium Farquhar had spent
four and a half years building.

93
00:06:04,325 --> 00:06:09,097
On the 1st of May 1823, Raffles
dismissed Farquhar as Resident,

94
00:06:09,177 --> 00:06:12,486
technically without the
authority to do so —

95
00:06:12,566 --> 00:06:16,725
only the Governor-General
in Bengal had appointed him.

96
00:06:16,725 --> 00:06:19,525
When John Crawfurd arrived to take over,

97
00:06:19,605 --> 00:06:23,925
Raffles dismissed Farquhar a
second time, now as Commandant.

98
00:06:23,925 --> 00:06:27,250
Farquhar appealed to Bengal and lost.

99
00:06:27,250 --> 00:06:31,642
Farquhar left Singapore on
the 28th of December 1823,

100
00:06:31,722 --> 00:06:36,700
dismissed twice over and
humiliated in the official record.

101
00:06:36,700 --> 00:06:40,790
The reception from the people
who'd actually lived under his

102
00:06:40,870 --> 00:06:45,516
administration told a different
story: the Bugis, Chinese and Indian

103
00:06:45,596 --> 00:06:48,991
communities gave him
emotional farewell addresses,

104
00:06:49,071 --> 00:06:54,075
and local merchants raised $3,000 to
have silverware made in his honour.

105
00:06:54,075 --> 00:06:57,550
The official record won out anyway.

106
00:06:57,550 --> 00:07:00,941
Raffles' 1897 biographer,
Demetrius Boulger,

107
00:07:01,021 --> 00:07:05,595
set the template that stuck —
Raffles as visionary founder,

108
00:07:05,675 --> 00:07:09,225
Farquhar reduced to a
footnote — and it held.

109
00:07:09,225 --> 00:07:12,770
Raffles got a statue at
his landing site and,

110
00:07:12,850 --> 00:07:16,475
eventually, an MRT
station carrying his name.

111
00:07:16,475 --> 00:07:19,338
Farquhar Street, Mount
Farquhar and Farquhar's

112
00:07:19,418 --> 00:07:22,425
Strait have all since
disappeared from the map.

113
00:07:22,425 --> 00:07:25,527
Modern historians — John
Bastin, Mary Turnbull,

114
00:07:25,607 --> 00:07:30,334
Ernest Chew among them — have pushed
back on the imbalance for decades,

115
00:07:30,414 --> 00:07:33,325
but the popular version
never really moved.

116
00:07:33,325 --> 00:07:36,315
Farquhar's own tombstone,
in Perth, Scotland,

117
00:07:36,395 --> 00:07:41,636
still describes him more plainly than
Singapore ever has: Resident of Malacca,

118
00:07:41,716 --> 00:07:46,150
and "afterwards at Singapore,
which later settlement he founded."

119
00:07:46,150 --> 00:07:49,470
Why it matters today:
Singapore's founding story

120
00:07:49,550 --> 00:07:53,224
gets told as the work of
one visionary Englishman who

121
00:07:53,304 --> 00:07:56,775
saw the island's potential
and built accordingly.

122
00:07:56,775 --> 00:08:00,894
The actual record shows something
messier and more interesting —

123
00:08:00,974 --> 00:08:05,356
a company officer who did the
physical work of founding a settlement

124
00:08:05,436 --> 00:08:09,884
for four and a half years, was
overruled and dismissed by the man who

125
00:08:09,964 --> 00:08:14,149
then took sole credit, and today
survives mostly as the name of a

126
00:08:14,229 --> 00:08:18,625
small garden at Fort Canning that
most visitors walk straight past.
